## Key decisions

- **WebSocket library: `github.com/coder/websocket`.** Context-aware,
  zero-dependency, net/http-native. Chosen over gorilla/websocket (no
  contexts, chattier API) and gobwas/ws (lower level than this needs).
- **Persistence: SQLite via `modernc.org/sqlite`**, pure Go, no cgo, with
  the single-writer/many-reader pool split — same as the sibling
  kikapcsologo backend, so the tooling and query conventions carry over.
- **Hand-written SQL in `internal/model/`**, no code generation.
- **Fan-out is per-channel, never global.** Each channel owns its member
  set and does its own broadcast; the hub's map is only touched on
  join/part/lookup.
- **Backpressure never propagates into the hub.** A full outbound buffer
  disconnects that client; it must not block the sender.
- **Wire protocol is `VERB {json}` text frames**, one command per frame.
- **Swappable `Broadcaster` interface in `internal/broadcast`.** The fan-out
  is the component most likely to be rewritten, so alternatives are added
  to one table and are measured by the existing tests and benchmarks
  without touching a caller.
- **Fan-out benchmarks report `drops/op` alongside `ns/op`.** A drop-on-full
  broadcaster can post a fantastic time by delivering to nobody; without
  that metric an invalid run is indistinguishable from a fast one.

